  "question": {
    "text": "Average distance of the Earth from the Sun is $L_{1}$ : If one year of the Earth $=D$ days, one year of another planet whose average distance from the Sun is $L_{2}$ will be"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$D\\left(\\frac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ days",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$D\\left(\\frac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}$ days",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$D\\left(\\frac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}}$ days",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$D\\left(\\frac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\\right)$ days",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "By Keplar's law,\r\n$$\r\n\\begin{array}{l}\r\nT^{2} \\propto R^{3} \\\\\r\nT=D\\left(\\frac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\\right)^{3 / 2}\r\n\\end{array}\r\n$$"
  }
}